Title: The Impact of U.S. Anti-Smoking Campaigns on Smoking Rates
For my final data analysis project, I want to explore whether major anti-smoking campaigns in the United States (such as the CDC’s "Tips from Former Smokers" campaign) were associated with declines in cigarette use among adults. My goal is to visualize national trends in smoking prevalence over time and highlight years when major campaigns were launched.
The data I would use includes:
# Load libraries
library(ggplot2)
# Create your fake dataset
fake_data <- data.frame(
Year = 2008:2018,
Smoking_Prevalence = c(20.6, 20.1, 19.5, 19.0, 18.0, 17.5, 16.8, 15.7, 15.5, 14.0, 13.7),
Campaign = c("No", "No", "No", "No", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes")
)
write.csv(fake_data, "fake_smoking_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
write.csv(fake_data, "Assignment_4/fake_smoking_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
write.csv(fake_data, "Assignment_4/fake_smoking_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
setwd("~/Data_Course_BASIM/Data_Course")
